<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gjirokastër</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Argyrokastron, Gjirokastra, Argyrokastro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gjirokastër is a town in southern Albania, built on a mountainside above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drino">Drino</a> valley and dominated by a large castle. It was taken around 1386 by the Albanian chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-zenebishi">Gjon Zenebishi</a> and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1419. Its stone tower houses of the Ottoman period earned it a place on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 2005. It was the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> and the writer Ismail Kadare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gjirokaster.jpg" alt="Gjirokastër" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Name: Armin Flickr: flickr.com/octopuzz · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gjirokaster_2016-2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
